  "body": "{\"model\":\"muse-spark-1.2-contributor\",\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"<system-reminder>\\nAs you answer the user's questions, you can use the following context:\\n# claudeMd\\nCodebase and user instructions are shown below. Be sure to adhere to these instructions. IMPORTANT: These instructions OVERRIDE any default behavior and you MUST follow them exactly as written.\\n\\nContents of /Users/barock/Second-Brain/CLAUDE.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\\n\\n# Second Brain — LLM Wiki Schema\\n\\nThis is a personal knowledge base maintained by an LLM, following the LLM Wiki pattern. The LLM writes and maintains the wiki; the human curates sources and directs exploration.\\n\\n## Role on the VPS\\n\\nSecond-Brain is the **mother workspace** for every Claude Code session on this VPS. The user mostly drives sessions via Telegram (`claude-code` channel), so the cwd at session start is always this repo. Treat it as the default home base:\\n\\n- New initiative with no repo yet → create `workspace/<slug>/` and work there.\\n- Initiative already has its own repo → `cd` into it; Second-Brain stays the mother and the place knowledge lands afterward.\\n- Knowledge-capture asks (ingest, query, lint, synthesize) → work in `raw/` + `wiki/` as defined below.\\n\\nWhen a session ends, valuable findings should land back in `wiki/` even if the work itself lived in `workspace/<slug>/` or another repo.\\n\\n## Directory Structure\\n\\n```\\nSecond-Brain/\\n├── CLAUDE.md          # This file — schema and conventions\\n├── llm-wiki.md        # Blueprint document (reference only)\\n├── raw/               # Source documents (immutable — never modify)\\n│   ├── assets/        # Images and attachments\\n│   └── ...            # Articles, papers, notes, transcripts\\n├── wiki/              # LLM-generated pages (LLM owns this entirely)\\n│   ├── index.md       # Content catalog — updated on every ingest\\n│   ├── log.md         # Chronological operation log\\n│   └── ...            # Entity, concept, source, synthesis pages\\n└── workspace/         # Ephemeral per-initiative scratch (gitignored)\\n    └── <slug>/        # One dir per initiative; freeform contents\\n```\\n\\n## Conventions\\n\\n### Page Types\\n- **Source pages** (`wiki/src-*.md`): Summary of an ingested source. Frontmatter includes: title, author, date, source type, tags.\\n- **Entity pages** (`wiki/ent-*.md`): People, organizations, tools, products. Updated when new sources mention them.\\n- **Concept pages** (`wiki/con-*.md`): Ideas, frameworks, patterns. Cross-referenced with related concepts and entities.\\n- **Comparison pages** (`wiki/cmp-*.md`): Side-by-side analyses.\\n- **Synthesis pages** (`wiki/syn-*.md`): Cross-cutting analyses drawing from multiple sources.\\n\\n### Frontmatter\\nEvery wiki page uses YAML frontmatter:\\n```yaml\\n---\\ntitle: Page Title\\ntype: source | entity | concept | comparison | synthesis\\ntags: [tag1, tag2]\\ncreated: 2026-04-07\\nupdated: 2026-04-07\\nsources: [src-filename1, src-filename2]\\n---\\n```\\n\\n### Cross-References\\nUse standard markdown links: `[[page-name]]` for Obsidian compatibility, or `[display text](page-name.md)` for portable markdown.\\n\\n### Naming\\n- Filenames: lowercase, hyphenated, prefixed by type (`src-`, `ent-`, `con-`, `cmp-`, `syn-`)\\n- Keep names short but descriptive\\n\\n## Workflows\\n\\n### Ingest\\n1. Read the new source in `raw/`\\n2. Discuss key takeaways with the user (if interactive)\\n3. Create a source summary page in `wiki/`\\n4. Create or update relevant entity and concept pages\\n5. Add cross-references between pages\\n6. Update `wiki/index.md`\\n7. Append entry to `wiki/log.md`\\n\\n### Query\\n**Wiki-first rule:** always consult `wiki/index.md` + relevant pages **before** any external search (web, repo grep, MCP). Go outside only if the wiki is insufficient — then fold the new finding back into `wiki/`.\\n\\n1. Read `wiki/index.md` to find relevant pages\\n2. Read the relevant pages\\n3. If wiki covers it → synthesize an answer with citations. If not → search outside, then file the finding as a new wiki page\\n4. If the answer is valuable, file it as a new wiki page\\n\\n### Workspace (ephemeral initiatives)\\n`workspace/` is gitignored — contents live only on the VPS, never pushed. Rules:\\n\\n1. **Create** `workspace/<slug>/` for any task that needs scratch files, experiments, or multi-step build work without its own repo. Slug = short kebab-case (e.g. `vps-bootstrap`, `chatterbox-id-eval`).\\n2. **Always write `NOTES.md`** in the slug dir. This is the initiative's durable working memory — not optional. Keep it short and live: goal, current state, last decision, next step, open questions. Update as work progresses. This is what lets the session survive `/compact`, `/clear`, restarts, or being away for days.\\n3. **Stay inside the slug dir** — never spill initiative files into `raw/`, `wiki/`, or the repo root. Raw sources captured *during* the initiative still go to `raw/`; durable findings still go to `wiki/`. Workspace is for the in-progress mess.\\n4. **Discard freely** — anything in `workspace/` is assumed throwaway. Before deleting a slug dir, confirm with the user and make sure durable knowledge has been promoted to `wiki/`.\\n5. **Graduate to a repo** only when the user explicitly asks (\\\"graduate X\\\", \\\"make X its own repo\\\"). Manual flow: move `workspace/<slug>/` out of Second-Brain, `git init`, create remote, push. No script.\\n6. **Cross-link** — when a workspace initiative produces a wiki page, mention the slug in the page so the history is recoverable while the dir still exists.\\n\\n### Cloning third-party repos\\nWhen the user asks to clone an existing GitHub repo, place it inside a workspace slug — never at Second-Brain root, never in `raw/` or `wiki/`. Pick layout by intent:\\n\\n**1. Study / reference** (read code, learn pattern, extract snippets)\\n```\\nworkspace/<slug>/\\n├── NOTES.md           # why cloned, what investigating, findings\\n└── <repo-name>/       # the clone (use upstream name)\\n```\\n\\n**2. Clone as dep for an initiative** (build something using their code)\\n```\\nworkspace/<slug>/\\n├── NOTES.md\\n├── upstream/          # their repo, untouched — keeps `git status` clean\\n└── ...                # your scratch, scripts, outputs\\n```\\n\\n**3. Fork + modify + contribute back**\\n- Start exploratory inside `workspace/<slug>/<repo-name>/`.\\n- Once intent solidifies (going to PR, or taking ownership) → **graduate**: move the clone out of Second-Brain to its own dir, point at the user's fork remote. From then on it's a real repo, not workspace.\\n\\nRules:\\n- Nested `.git` inside `workspace/*/` is fine — `workspace/` is gitignored, no submodule confusion with Second-Brain's git.\\n- Durable findings about upstream code → promote to `wiki/` (e.g. `wiki/src-<repo>-architecture.md`) so knowledge survives slug-dir deletion. Cite the slug in the wiki page.\\n- Default clone depth: full unless user asks `--depth 1`. Always confirm the URL with the user before cloning.\\n\\n### Long-running session with multiple initiatives\\nThis Claude session runs continuously on the VPS, driven via Telegram. Many initiatives share one session. Two failure modes to avoid: context bloat, and cross-initiative contamination.\\n\\n**Slug = the topic anchor.** At any moment, exactly one initiative is \\\"active.\\\" When the user switches, you switch.\\n\\n**How the user signals an initiative (any of these):**\\n- `[slug] ...` prefix on a TG message.\\n- \\\"switch to <slug>\\\" / \\\"new initiative: <slug>\\\" / \\\"back to <slug>\\\".\\n- Naming the slug in passing when topic clearly changes.\\n\\n**On initiative switch:**\\n1. If the active initiative has unsaved state, flush it to `workspace/<active>/NOTES.md` first.\\n2. Suggest `/compact` (or `/clear` if context is heavy and unrelated). Wait for user OK before clearing.\\n3. `cat workspace/<new-slug>/NOTES.md` to rehydrate. If it doesn't exist, create the dir and seed NOTES.md from the user's prompt.\\n4. Confirm the active slug back to the user in one li"
